[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The problem was a [1mmissing FFI export[22m. The test runner dynamically loads [38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m (matching the C code's [38;5;10mSPX_NAMESPACE(gen_message_random)[0m macro which prepends [38;5;10mSPX_[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation had [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m as an internal [38;5;10mpub fn[0m in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, but it was never exported as an [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m function with the [38;5;10mSPX_[0m prefix. The [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m file only exported [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_*[0m functions.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix: I added a [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] pub extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(...)[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that:[0m[0m
1. Takes raw C pointers matching the expected signature [38;5;10mfn(*mut u8, *const u8, *const u8, *const u8, c_ulonglong, *const SpxCtx)[0m[0m[0m
2. Converts them to Rust slices[0m[0m
3. Delegates to the existing [38;5;10mhash::gen_message_random()[0m implementation[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The underlying BLAKE-512 hash implementation was actually correct — the only issue was the symbol not being visible to the dynamic linker. After adding the export, all test vectors pass (1/1).[0m[0m
